# Paths that may be committed to this repository, one glob per line; `#` starts a
# comment. The pre-commit hook refuses any staged path that no line here matches,
# so a path arrives in the repository only when this file says it may.
#
# Why, when ~/.gitignore already allows the same set: the work tree is the home
# directory, so that file decides what `git status` shows, which is a filter and
# not a guard. A path it does not cover yet, or one staged with `git add -f`,
# travels on the next commit unopposed. Hence the second list, enforced instead
# of displayed.
#
# `*` spans `/` as well, so `.config/nix/*` covers everything below it. Keep the
# lines as narrow as the content: `.dotfiles/*` would defeat the point of the file.

# Top-level files
.gitconfig
.gitignore
.gitmodules
.hidden
.vimrc
.zshenv
.zshrc

# Submodules: the gitlink itself, never a path inside them
.antigen
.fzf
.vim/bundle/Vundle.vim

# Keybindings for the agents, read from the home directory
.claude/keybindings.json

# The parts of ~/.config this repository tracks
.config/nix/*
.config/nvim/*

# GitHub reads the README through this symlink, and the licence sits next to it
.github/LICENSE
.github/README.md

# The dotfiles, directory by directory. A new file placed straight in
# ~/.dotfiles is refused on purpose: that is where machine-specific git
# settings used to land, one ignore rule away from being published.
.dotfiles/agents/*
.dotfiles/claude-plugins/*
.dotfiles/hooks/*
.dotfiles/nix/*
.dotfiles/nixos/*
.dotfiles/scripts/*
.dotfiles/vscodium/*
.dotfiles/zsh/*
.dotfiles/.gitignore_global
.dotfiles/AGENTS.md
.dotfiles/allowed-paths
.dotfiles/Brewfile
.dotfiles/LICENSE
.dotfiles/README.md
.dotfiles/TODO.md
